Cambodia Celebrates National Teachers' Day October 05, 2022 Under the Theme "Our Teachers, Our Future, Our Nation"
(Phnom Penh): The Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports this morning, October 5, 2022, celebrated the 25th anniversary of National Teachers' Day, October 5, 2022 under the theme "Our teachers, our future, our nation". To revive the culture and tradition of commemorating the noble merits of teachers across the country and for the general public to better understand the values of teachers and the meaning of this day.
The ceremony was presided over by Dr. Hang Chuon Naron, Minister of Education, Youth and Sports, and was attended by leaders, educators, students, teachers of educational institutions in collaboration with relevant ministries and institutions through the system. Online and in person at Phnom Penh Pedagogical Institute.
At the same time, to celebrate the National Teachers' Day, the Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports also organized a smot-poetry program, a flag-awarding ceremony for 5 high school administrators and a Monisaraphorn Medal for 11 educators. Participate with students from all over the capital to inspire, thank teachers and improve education at all levels.
Hang Chuon Naron added that the Kovid-19 crisis has made it clear that teachers are the engine of the education system, teachers are the heart of education. The role and effectiveness of teachers is very important to ensure that students and students receive an education in all circumstances with equity, environment and quality.
Teachers also have an important role to play in reviving education from the Kovid-19 crisis and to determine the future of all students. Therefore, we all need to improve teachers, improve teaching and the teaching profession to achieve a better future for Cambodian children and youth.
The minister added that during Kovid-19, there was also a loss of teachers, which disrupted the learning process due to the loss of education, especially in disadvantaged areas. But Cambodia is really proud of the fact that teachers have worked hard to learn new skills, such as teaching online.
The Minister further asserted that in response to the challenges of Kovid 19, the Royal Government of Cambodia, as well as the Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports, has set out and is accelerating the implementation of effective and gentle measures to ensure the sustainability of education and operations. Re-establish the educational institution through the re-opening step by step and be careful, especially focusing on teaching partners such as:
Priority 1: Ensure the safe reopening of schools, the restoration of learning and the continuation of successful learning. Education in the "new normal path" must ensure safety, health, including mental health for educators and students.
Priority 2: School reforms that focus on accountability, autonomy, evaluation, and parent and stakeholder involvement.
Priority 3: Development of high quality teachers through the reform of teacher training institutions by improving teacher training, teacher training and professional development on a regular basis, as well as investing in the necessary infrastructure and teaching materials.
Priority 4: Digital education focuses on the development of digital platforms, the production of instructional videos, the integration of technology into teaching and learning, and the development of digital infrastructure.
Priority 5: Educate science, technology, engineering and mathematics in response to the 4.0 Industrial Revolution by investing in a new generation of schools, Stem teachers, Stem teaching methods and 21st century skills.
Priority 6: Developing 21st Century Skills for Youth Focus on: (1) Hard and soft skills that focus on 21st century skills such as critical thinking, problem solving, communication, teamwork, and Leadership (2) Vocational training programs linked to job opportunities, productivity and competition; (3) Youth participation in economic and social development.
Priority 7: Establishment of the Center of Excellence at the tertiary level by strengthening the governance of higher education institutions, improving the qualifications of professors, research and innovation, establishing the University of Cyber, and linking the labor market.
Priority 8: Strengthening institutional capacity and building an education system to turn the Kovid-19 crisis into an opportunity for reform and improvement of the education system to be resilient to future epidemics.
