General Knowledge: "KGB", a well-known intelligence agency of the former Soviet Union during the Cold War
During the Cold War, the KGB was responsible for overseas espionage and domestic security. In addition, the KGB was responsible for protecting Soviet political leaders, controlling troops at the border, and conducting general surveillance of the Soviet people.
A year later, the GPU was renamed the "OGPU" and took on additional responsibilities, such as managing labor camps and monitoring population activity. During the early 1930s, the OGPU began conducting covert operations on foreign territory to curb the activities of groups opposed to the Soviet Union from the outside. Some of them were kidnapped and murdered.
During World War II, the MGB carried out various espionage operations and also focused on countering enemy espionage, plus overseeing the concentration camps of war prisoners and ensuring loyalty among the military. After the war, the MGB helped the Soviet Union suppress opposition movements, whether true or false, in Eastern Europe and in the Soviet Union itself.
Russian President Vladimir Putin served as the KGB's foreign intelligence official for 16 years before resigning in 1991 to pursue a career as a politician.
