Siem Reap Province Holds Discussion on Waste and Solid Waste Management in Tourist Cities in ASEAN Countries, Siem Reap Province Has Increased Waste to 160 Million Tons Since 2006
(Siem Reap): The Siem Reap Provincial Administration has opened discussions on waste management, solid waste and solid waste in tourist cities in ASEAN countries. Laos and Dalat, Vietnam are pilot cities for sustainable governance.
The steady increase in population in major cities poses major challenges for public administration management. Garbage is a growing solid waste in ASEAN member states that lack resources and infrastructure.
On that occasion, on October 4, 2022, Mr. Khun Linne, Deputy Governor of Siem Reap Province, said that every year, the waste has increased to 160 million tons since 2006 for solid waste in the tourist city.
Mr. Khun Linne, Deputy Governor of Siem Reap, also thanked the German Cooperation Project for choosing Siem Reap to implement this waste management project.
Meanwhile, Frank Jattke, Director of the German Cooperation Project (GIZ), stated that the project also aims to contribute to improving the environmental quality of medium-sized tourism cities in the ASEAN region by strengthening the capacity of Save on final waste and efficient recycling.
The project is also funded by the Federal Ministry for Economic and Development Cooperation, implemented by the German Cooperation Agency in close cooperation with the ASEAN Secretariat.
He added that the project also contributes to the achievement of ASEAN goals as set out in the ASEAN Socio-Cultural Community Master Plan 2025 and to increase the efficiency of solid waste management, sustainable recycling and considering the role of gender. In the destination tourist city.
