The Minister of Labor requested the relevant ministries and institutions to examine the possibility of preparing the necessary regulations so that Cambodia can eliminate the use of asbestos in the future.
The request of the Minister of Labor was made during the launch of the National Asbestos Bulletin, organized by the Ministry of Labor and Vocational Training under the collaboration and co-sponsorship of Afida and ILO / Japan through the project to improve occupational health and safety standards in the construction industry in Cambodia, held at the Phnom Penh Hotel on the morning of October 4, 2022.
At the same time, the Minister thanked the working group to build the National Asbestos Information Network of the Kingdom of Cambodia for its efforts to collect information and reports from relevant ministries and institutions that directly surveyed construction companies.
The minister pointed out that by 2021, 67 countries had banned the use of asbestos, using safer alternatives.
As for Pablo Kang, the Australian Ambassador to Cambodia, he said that asbestos has a serious impact on human health, such as causing lung and throat cancer through the use of various substances. Unconscious. While some people claim that the use of this substance is not harmful to health, but it is completely contrary to their thinking.
The Ambassador hopes that through today's outreach program, all participants will be encouraged to pay more attention to the study and study to reduce the use of asbestos. National Committee for Disaster Management and Development Partners Organizes Workshop on Disaster Financial Preparation and Transportation Rehabilitation
