In August 2022, the Royal Government of Cambodia, through an inter-ministerial commission led by Deputy Prime Minister Chea Sophara, Minister of Land Management, Urban Planning and Construction, launched a large-scale campaign to demarcate plots in Angkor and other areas. Also under the control of the APSARA Authority, as well as the removal of illegal structures from these areas.
According to the report of the Ministry of Land Management, Urban Planning and Construction, the operation to preserve Angkor as a human resource in the world has achieved the measurement and extraction of land plots occupied by people in the Angkor resort area in 114 villages, 24 communes / Sangkat and 5 cities / districts equal to 97,855 plots of land size of 29,174 hectares.
At the same time, a total of 3,868 families who volunteered to demolish illegal structures from the Angkor resort area and other areas under the control of the APSARA Authority were selected to relocate to the Ronta Ek Development Zone. People volunteered and dismantled about 400 illegal stalls, sheds and houses in the Angkor resort area.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen, in a meeting with volunteers living in Run Ta Ek village, expressed his commitment to turn Run Ta Ek village from a forest area into a development area and intends to make Run Ta Ek village into a town.
