IMF says Ukraine war triggers worst global food crisis since 2008 (Video inside) To have the worst food security crisis since at least the global financial crisis of 2007-2008, with about 345 million people now facing life-threatening shortages.
Forty-eight countries facing the worst food shortages are expected to face a total increase of $ 9 billion in import spending in 2022 and 2023, Reuters reported, citing a new IMF research report. The sharp rise in food and fertilizer prices caused by Russia's invasion of Ukraine.
The IMF says this will destroy the reserves of many countries that are already fragile and affected by conflicts already facing a balance of payments crisis after a devastating epidemic and an increase. Energy prices.
The war has exacerbated the food crisis since 2018, in part due to rising frequency and severity of climate change and regional conflicts, officials said.
The IMF has called for an immediate increase in humanitarian assistance through the World Food Program and other organizations, as well as targeted fiscal measures in affected countries to help the poor. But the fund says the government needs to prioritize the fight against inflation. According to Reuters.
