SEOUL: South Korean, US and Japanese navies on Friday launched their first joint anti-submarine drills in five years amid tensions over test firing. North Korea's series of missiles. This was released by the AFP news agency on Friday, September 30, 2022.
The above tripartite military exercises are taking place in international waters near the east coast of the Korean Peninsula. It comes just a day after North Korea fired two more ballistic missiles into the sea east of its country, and US Vice President Kamala Harris recently visited. In Seoul, as well as traveled to the inter-Korean border area.
This week's test is the third missile test fired by North Korea in just five days, while in 2022 Pyongyang has conducted more missile tests than ever before. The South Korean navy said the exercise was aimed at strengthening joint response capabilities to the growing threat of North Korean submarines, including SLBM submarine-launched ballistic missiles.
Anti-submarine military exercises have not been held between the three countries since 2017 as the former South Korean government sought to improve inter-Korean relations and facilitate coordination. Nuclear disarmament talks between Pyongyang and Washington have stalled since 2019.
