According to a report released Thursday by four leading German research institutes, Germany's economic output this year and next will be lower than the five-year forecast, Bloomberg reported on Thursday. Last month, 160 billion euros ($ 154 billion), in part due to a sharp rise in energy prices.
Germany is one of the countries most affected by the energy crisis caused by the Ukraine war due to the dependence on fuel imports from Russia that have developed over the decades. The incumbent government of Chancellor Olaf Scholz is trying to reduce its reliance on Russian energy, but Germany still faces a difficult winter if it lacks gas for heating.
The institutes also forecast that Germany, Europe's largest economy, will return to growth in 2024 with a growth rate of 1.9%.
