(Phnom Penh): After a two-year absence due to the 19-Kovid epidemic, on the morning of Thursday, September 29, 2022, the Royal Government of Cambodia has re-organized the "National Interfaith Forum Against Human Trafficking." The forum was held under the theme "Do not take Cambodia as a target for human trafficking" held under the chairmanship of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, and with the participation of many other relevant officials.
Since 2017, the National Committee for Combating Human Trafficking, chaired by Deputy Prime Minister and Minister of Interior Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng, has been working with major Cambodian religious leaders to organize a national interfaith forum to pray for the Cambodian people. As well as the people of the world are happy to avoid the abuse and human trafficking of modern parliament.
The importance of this program is to show the harmony of religion in Cambodia, which always agrees to cooperate and support each other with gentleness, without conflict, without quarreling, without causing division and disaster in society. On the contrary, the harmony of religion has played a key role in building individual peace of mind and social security, as well as helping to strengthen the spirit of all people together to combat the incitement of human trafficking criminals.
The 6th National Interfaith Forum 2022 will be attended by Buddhist, Muslim, Catholic, Christian, Protestant and other believers, as well as representatives of a number of national and international organizations working on the fight against human trafficking in Cambodia. Cambodia, about 1,500 units / person.
Please note that in recent months, Cambodia has been operating vigorously to crack down on human trafficking, labor trafficking, especially in gambling places.
Since the start of the operation in August, police have identified nearly 300 cases and rescued victims of human trafficking who have been subjected to sanctions, closures and exploitation of hundreds of workers. More people are leaving the secret locations, and measures are being taken to close the crime scene.
Over the past year, Cambodia has investigated 178 out of more than 300 complaints, found 361 victims and arrested 53 perpetrators. Authorities closed one company and suspended business, as well as continuing to search five companies. This is according to the report of the Ministry of Interior.
The result is a clear demonstration of the Royal Government of Cambodia's spirit in the fight against human trafficking and labor trafficking.
