(Siem Reap): The Department of Water Management, Forests and Infrastructure of the APSARA Authority has been working to release water through sluices and some temple areas to prevent the temple from flooding.
The effects of Typhoon Nuro from the Philippines have been causing low pressure and continuous rain in Cambodia since mid-September. This caused several rains and flooded some districts in Siem Reap. At the Angkor resort, heavy rains were collected and some were even released due to excessive amounts.
According to a brief report from the Department of Water Management, Forests and Infrastructure of the APSARA Authority, the team released water from some temples such as Angkor Wat, Preah Khan, Ta Prohm and Bayon. Avoid water through the traditional canal system along rivers such as Stung Siem Reap, Stung Rolous, Stung Puok and Stung Preah Srok so as not to have a negative impact on the temple area and Siem Reap city.
In addition to the above work, the team also collected water to fill the traditional management system to ensure the balance of water use in the Angkor area, such as the western baray, the northern baray, Angkor Thom, Angkor Thom, Angkor Wat, Angkor Wat, Srah Srang and Kasin Preah Khan. . General Vong Pisen Sends Condolences on the Death of Senior Buddhist Disciple Tun Thi
