Senior Minister Kun Kim Continues to Call on Authorities and Residents in Provinces to Be Aware of Rain
(Phnom Penh): Senior Minister Kun Kim, First Deputy Chairman of the National Committee for Disaster Management, Samdech Techo Hun Sen's staff on disaster work, continues to closely monitor the flood situation, especially in the northwestern provinces after Providing information related to the continued impact of rainfall in those provinces.
Northwestern provinces such as Oddar Meanchey, Banteay Meanchey and Siem Reap, which are experiencing mild flooding due to flooding and waterfall from Thailand, may face flooding with more rainfall and runoff from Thailand in Between September 27 and 30.
As the operator of the Disaster Management Battlefield, Senior Minister Kun Kim has been closely monitoring this new natural phenomenon. In addition to all operations, the National Committee for Disaster Management is also ready to respond immediately in the event of severe flooding.
"Under the strict orders of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, we are all ready, both operational and safe. "We are following the latest developments on natural phenomena, according to the Ministry of Water Resources and Meteorology." This was confirmed by Senior Minister Kun Kim.
However, Senior Minister Kun Kim called on the disaster management secretaries in the provinces facing the effects of the rains to be ready 24 hours a day to rescue people in case of severe flooding.
The senior minister appealed, "Provincial disaster management secretaries, especially in all provinces facing floods, must be ready 24 hours a day to help the people." Remember the words of the Prime Minister, do not let any people be harmed by the authorities do not know.
In addition to appealing to the authorities, Senior Minister Kun Kim also called on people in flood-prone areas to be prepared to evacuate to safer areas and to raise property to a higher level to avoid flooding. The senior minister also advised residents to contact authorities immediately when their homes are threatened by flooding. Be especially careful not to let small children play in the water and risk drowning.
On this occasion, Senior Minister Kun Kim, who regularly monitors and inspects the flooded areas, confirmed that the flood situation for several days has not yet reached a serious level compared to the floods in 2020. He said the floodwaters were receding so some residents could stay on their homes while others were evacuated to safety.
Meanwhile, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Chairman of the National Committee for Disaster Management, has already provided food to about 500 vulnerable families in Banteay Meanchey and Oddar Meanchey provinces. In addition, the Cambodian Red Cross, National Reserve, as well as the director of the Secretariat of the Provincial Disaster Management Commission have organized a series of food distribution to vulnerable people.
