Opinion: Many temples, good temples, but Buddhists do not understand the Dharma as if the body has lost its soul
Apart from those who travel to the temple, some may also be traveling to their hometown to reunite with their families and possibly to the temple on Pchum Day. To travel to this hometown, some people use cars, some motorbikes, some tricycles.
On either side of the national road to Takeo province, there are a large number of temples and places of worship of the Dharma of Samma Samput. In some places, the gap is only about 200 to 300 meters.
These monasteries also have large courtyards, large and beautiful temples that should be a paradise and a clear place for worship and worship of the Dharma. Through the presence of so many temples, this is a manifestation of the prosperity and growth of Buddhism, our national religion.
The glory of Buddhism brings great joy and admiration for the devotees of the Dharma of Samma Samput and who used to send destiny to the temple for service. The pagoda in the Kan Ben festival, whether old or young, go to the pagoda, follow each other, give some food to the monks, count the sand mounds and leave the pagoda.
On the occasion of Kan Ben or Pchum Ben, because it is a Buddhist festival, but in the home, they do not see a ceremony that enhances the understanding of the Dharma of the Buddha. Instead, it is replaced by gatherings, eating, singing, and dancing, which are contrary to the content of the Dharma.
When asked about the worship of Kanben or Pchum Ben, it is a tragedy that some Buddhists say that they follow the elders, but do not understand the true meaning of the ceremony. It is not Ben or Bon Pchum that the Khmer Buddhists are not very knowledgeable, in that the content of the Dharma of many monks, the Khmer Buddhists are not very knowledgeable or can say that there are not many scholars.
Among Cambodian Buddhists, there is often a conflict of opinion on the worship of the Dharma of the Master. Disagreements or conflicts can come from not understanding or not fully understanding the Dharma of the Samma Samputa.
Looking back at the temple, we see that although there is a lot of growth in the village, but it does not seem to play a role as a place to educate the Khmer people as before. The temple is only a place for worshiping the Dharma of grandparents, while young or young people go to the pagoda only when there are big traditional ceremonies such as Pchum, New Year and so on. In particular, even Vesak, a major festival in Buddhism, is rarely attended by young people.
On the other hand, in the temples, we see that there is a lot of construction of temples, halls and other achievements. But there seems to be a lack of space to build or set up a place to keep the rules of the scriptures, the decrees, the Dharma or the Trinity.
Many monasteries have good temples, which should be clear for worship, but should not be overlooked to strengthen the understanding of his Dharma. Many temples, beautiful temples, but lack of understanding of the Dharma is no different from having a body, but the spirit and soul are gone.
The Dharma of Preah Samma Samput has contributed significantly to the moral education in living away from the wickedness of the Cambodian people in addition to the laws of the state.
Therefore, consideration and attention should be given to strengthening the Dharma education of the Samma Samputa. Monasteries should increase the presentation of rules, scriptures, and dharma orders as much as possible. There should be a program to provide knowledge of the Dharma to the Khmer people in schools, temples and villages. Dr. Men Sam An: Throughout Svay Rieng province, 246 pagodas received relics and suffixes for construction on the occasion of Pchum Ben
