The Phnom Penh-Sihanoukville Expressway, which will be open to the public on October 1, 2022, has residents expressing concern about difficult driving at night due to the lack of street lighting.
Senior Minister Sun Chanthol, Minister of Public Works and Transport, claimed that as a standard, highways in the world do not have lights, as doing so would add to the cost of electricity. But instead of lighting, they set up a reflective system on the road that makes driving less difficult.
To see more clearly the reflection system as well as the actual situation of driving at night? On the night of September 22, 2022, Senior Minister Sun Chanthol led the Ministry's leadership as well as a group of journalists on an overnight trip across the expressway from Sihanoukville to Phnom Penh.
With night travel, we became aware of the night driving situation on this expressway and also provided a new experience with driving on the freeway for the first time in Cambodia at night.
To facilitate night driving, the expressway construction company has set up a well-reflected system on the road. They have embedded reflective systems on two roads, and also embedded reflectors on sidewalk fences and metal railings on park divisions.
The four-wire reflection system on both lanes creates a clear reflection when in contact with the headlights. So the driver can see the situation on the road clearly in the distance without causing difficulties for the driver.
Drivers who drove on the freeway on the night of September 22 have all demonstrated the technique of setting up the reflection system that really makes it easier to drive because it makes it easier to see the road conditions. Do not drive even if there are no street lights on the side of the road or on either side of the road.
Please note that the Phnom Penh-Sihanoukville Expressway project has a length of 187.05 km, construction started on March 22, 2019. This road connects Village 1, Sangkat Samrong Krom, Khan Por Senchey, Phnom Penh to Village 1, Sangkat 3, Sihanoukville, Preah Sihanouk Province.
This historic road in Cambodia is almost complete and will be launched in October with a one-month free charge.
The Phnom Penh-Sihanoukville Expressway is the first expressway project and the most important strategic corridor project in Cambodia, reducing travel time from Phnom Penh to Sihanoukville seaport from five hours to just two hours; Reduces logistics costs as well.
With day and night inspections, Senior Minister Sun Chanthol praised that the expressways in Cambodia are well built and technically correct. Compared to the highways in the countries he has traveled to, the highways in Cambodia are unmatched in quality and technology.
