According to Dr. Kao Kim Hourn, Deputy Prime Minister, the Prime Minister of Cambodia and Bangladesh discussed a number of issues related to strengthening cooperation and trade relations between the two countries. There is also talk of importing rice from Cambodia to Bangladesh.
In the past, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Bangladesh, negotiated to buy about 200,000 tons of rice from Cambodia. But the prince wants a stable import of rice from Cambodia to Bangladesh.
The Prime Minister also invited a Bangladeshi company to Cambodia to invest in a rice processing warehouse as a rice mill and export to Bangladesh.
The Prime Minister of Bangladesh informed Samdech Techo that she had asked the private sector to invest in Cambodia in the agricultural sector. Especially rice export from Cambodia.
She also told Samdech Techo that Bangladesh has small plots of land and many people are not suitable for farming due to the risk of floods, so they need to buy rice from outside. Through this, she suggested the idea of having an agreement with each other to expand and strengthen business between Cambodia and Bangladesh more.
She also expressed a desire to push for trade agreements between the two countries to increase exports and increase investment opportunities.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen agrees with the intention of the Prime Minister of Bangladesh. Samdech Techo said that the agreement is a work in which the two countries can start negotiations for a free trade agreement together to boost trade between the two countries, while Cambodia also has a free trade agreement with China and South Korea. Gems, too.
Samdech Techo expressed confidence that through this free trade agreement, Cambodia and Bangladesh can increase trade volume, which is an opportunity for Bangladesh to expand and strengthen relations with ASEAN.
In the above meeting, Samdech Techo Hun Sen thanked the Prime Minister of Bangladesh for sending the Minister of Foreign Affairs of Bangladesh to attend the ASEAN Regional Forum in Phnom Penh in early August. On behalf of the ASEAN Chair, Samdech Techo Hun Sen also thanked Bangladesh for providing 1 million doses of Kovid 19 vaccine to Myanmar.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen also thanked the Prime Minister for inaugurating a road in the name of His Majesty King Norodom Sihanouk in Bangladesh, and Cambodia officially named her father in honor of her father. The founder of Bangladesh was the first president and later became the Prime Minister.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen also congratulated Bangladesh on building a long bridge under her own leadership. Named the "Patma Bridge", it is more than six kilometers long and can be used by both cars and trains and uses only Bangladeshi budget. Mr. HUN Many: The verdict sentencing Mr. Khieu Samphan is a true reflection of the brutal regime that Cambodia has experienced in the past
(Phnom Penh): The Extraordinary Chambers in the Courts of Cambodia (Khmer Rouge Tribunal) on 22 September 2022 announces Judgment on Case 002/02 appeal against accused Khieu Samphan, former President of the President Of the Khmer Rouge regime. In this verdict, the Khmer Rouge tribunal upheld its decision, sentenced Khieu Samphan to life in prison and blocked the protest.
In addition, Mr. HUN Many considered that today is also dedicated to the souls of all the victims who lost their lives, as well as the reconciliation of emotional trauma for those who survived to live in pain. Calm down through the punishment of the accused appropriate to the acts they committed and ordered during the Khmer Rouge regime.
Mr. Hun Many described: “The historic day of the closing of the criminal trials that took place during the Khmer Rouge regime.
The final verdict announced by the Supreme Court Chamber on the morning of 22 September 2022 convicts former Democratic Head of State Khieu Samphan of the maximum sentence under the law of the Kingdom of Cambodia: life imprisonment in the case 002/02 shall be applied in conjunction with the sentence of life imprisonment in Case 002/01.
This verdict is also a fact that reflects the brutal regime that Cambodia experienced in the dark past. Through a process that has taken more than 16 years, justice has been formally granted, recognizing the existence of crime and ending the impunity of the leaders and most responsible of this criminal regime.
Not only that, today is also a day dedicated to the souls of all the victims who lost their lives, as well as the reconciliation of the traumatic victims for those who survived to live in peace through the tragedy. The sentence of the accused is commensurate with the act he committed and ordered.
"The next generation of young Cambodians must remember and learn from this history, not to allow the cracks of the past to recur, and to work together to maintain this peace, which is the basis for building national development."
Minister of Commerce: There should be impartial and qualified judges to resolve trade disputes
(Bali, Indonesia): The Minister of Commerce, Mr. Pan Sosak, said that there should be impartial and qualified judges to resolve trade disputes properly.
He made the remarks at an informal ministerial consultation on the reform of the WTO dispute settlement system on September 21, 2022 in Bali, Indonesia.
Mr. Pan Sosak said that technical assistance and legal advice must be provided to the least developed countries to assist in various cases related to this group of countries. In addition, there should be impartial and qualified judges from all regions and genders, and competent judges from less developed member countries should be encouraged.
The Minister informed that Cambodia is ready to actively participate with all stakeholders in the reform of the WTO Dispute Resolution System to make the system work better. In the future. Commentary: Kan Ben Festival Pchum Ben is linked to education in Cambodian society
(Phnom Penh): The Kan Ben and Pchum Ben festivals are always held from the 1st of Roach to the 15th of Roach Kheap every year and are also one of the largest festivals of all the festivals for Buddhists in Cambodia.
This year's Kan Ben and Pchum Ben festivals started on the 1st of Roach to the 15th of Roach, and the 11th to the 25th of September. During the days of Kan Ben, we observed that in the pagodas, both in the city and in the countryside, it was busier than usual, with Buddhists, young and old, men and women, bringing food to the monks in order to dedicate themselves. To all the relatives who have passed away to the next world.
Preah Somethavong Dr. Sok Bunthoeun, Preah Chao Athikar Wat Non Mony, once said that the celebration of Pchum Ben is very important because it shows gratitude to the rich people and Khmer ancestors who passed away. And the other part is to include education, counseling, teaching to men and women of all ages to be honest, listen to the deeds that leave the unfortunate deeds committed in daily life.
Voice: "When it comes to Buddhism, one misses education. Buddhism is a religion that plays a very important role in educating the body, speech, mind of our people to build something good for themselves as well as for society. Don't Pchum Ben ceremony is done to educate or disseminate education to the masses, both young and old, men and women.
The Venerable Buddha continued, "The most important mainstay of education in the Kan Ben and Pchum Ben ceremonies is the understanding of the causes of the destruction of the 12 things. In the morning, the monks of all pagodas regularly recite 15 days of the festival.
Professor Prak Samphors, a Buddhist professor at Sihanoukville University, said: As Buddhists who have respected the traditions from ancient times to the present, during the Pchum Ben festival, Buddhists need to do two important things:
The most common is to support the rich and give food or money to the gods to go to the pagoda to keep the morality in the pagoda. Fourth is the saving of merits for the present and the future. Fifth, increase unity because the Kan Ben ceremony is scheduled to start and end the same throughout the country, showing harmony. Sixth, to preserve the Khmer tradition, such as wearing the traditional clothes of our Cambodian women.
