Russian men flee country to avoid mobilization
MOSCOW (Reuters) - Russian men are fleeing the country, causing congestion at border crossings as they try to avoid a conspiracy to go to war in Ukraine. This is according to the publication by the BBC on Friday, September 23, 2022.
A man, who asked not to be named, told the BBC he had taken his passport and hurried to the border with nothing immediately after President Putin's announcement. Used to be in a group that could be sent to war.
However, the Kremlin claims that reports of the escape of adult fighting men are exaggerated. But in reality, along the Russian border with Georgia, there are hundreds of kilometers of cars, including men trying to escape the war.
On Wednesday, Russian President Vladimir Putin announced a military move to Ukraine, recruiting 300,000 people to serve on Russian citizens who are reserve or have served in the military.
