Finland claims increased traffic along Russia border after mobilization
(Helsinki) - Traffic to the eastern border of the country bordering Russia has increased significantly, but the situation is manageable, the Finnish border guards said on Thursday. This is according to the publication by Reuters on Thursday, September 22, 2022.
Following the Russian president's mobilization announcement on Wednesday, there were fears that some adult fighting men were not allowed to leave the country, leading to out-of-flight flights from Russia. Quickly. Angkor's largest Sivaling sculpture to be restored
(Siem Reap): The largest Sivaling sculpture in the Angkor area, along with the pedestals and structures of this ancient building, will be restored to enhance the beauty and heritage value. This statue of Sivaling is kept on Phnom Bok, Banteay Srei district, Siem Reap province today.
Currently, a team from the Department of Temple Preservation and Archeology is excavating and studying the structure before proceeding to plan for the restoration of the archeological site.
Hour Sothoun, an archaeologist at the APSARA Authority, said that the Sivaling on Phnom Bok was made of laterite, 20 by 19 meters long and 3.5 meters high, and there were sandstone pedestals on the pedestal. It is 5.2 meters long and square for holding a penis with a diameter of more than 1 meter and a length of 4.2 meters.
Mr. Hour Sothoun concluded that the serious damage may have been caused by the bombing because the team found many small pieces scattered all over the ground and on the ground.
Later, the laterite was damaged by natural age due to decay when the rock fell down in some corners. Which is prone to rainwater runoff, while termite mounds cause partial collapse and lack of previous maintenance.
He also confirmed that excavations at the site began in early August 2022 and the research project will be completed by the end of September 2022.
There are no inscriptions or documents confirming the date of construction on Mount Bok, but according to the inscriptions on the pedestal, it can be concluded that it may be. During the 10th century during the reign of King Yasovarman I.
