Cambodia, Malaysia rescue 153 victims from fraudulent workplace
(Phnom Penh): The Royal Government of Cambodia, in cooperation with the Malaysian Embassy in Phnom Penh, rescued 153 Malaysian victims from fraudulent workplaces, of which 137 were repatriated while 16 others were being repatriated. Followed by.
Elden said the 153 Malaysian victims were the victims of cyber-fraud, which lured them to work in Cambodia under the promise of a salary of 2-3 thousand dollars a month.
The Ambassador added that the rescue of the 153 Malaysians lasted only one month under the careful cooperation of the Royal Government of Cambodia, the Ministry of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation, the Ministry of Interior, as well as officials. Cambodian authorities and authorities with the Malaysian Embassy in Cambodia.
As for the means to bring them out of detention, the arrangement of accommodation and food, and cooperation with the Cambodian side to repatriate them, it is entirely at the expense of the Malaysian side. The victims were brought from Sihanoukville to a temporary accommodation at a hotel near the Boeung Kak Mosque before being sent to Phnom Penh International Airport to return home.
According to the ambassador, 137 victims have so far been repatriated, while 16 others are under the jurisdiction of the General Department of Immigration of the Ministry of Interior to complete the procedure before being allowed to leave. They return to their homeland.
(Phnom Penh): In the last five years, 258 artifacts have been returned to Cambodia after being lost during the war. This is according to Mr. Long Bonna Sirivath, Secretary of State and Spokesperson of the Ministry of Culture and Fine Arts in the press conference of the Spokesperson Unit of the Royal Government on the morning of September 22, 2022.
Mr. Long Ponna Sereywat stated that the artifacts returned to Cambodia included 33 from the United States, 71 from Japan, 110 from Hungary, 16 from Britain and 28 from Thailand. He claimed that the effort to bring back the lost Cambodian artifacts was led by Ms. Phoeung Sakona, Minister of Culture and Fine Arts.
The spokesman for the Ministry of Culture added that the demand for the return of Cambodian artifacts uses three main mechanisms, including:
* First: Voluntary appeals to those whose Cambodian artefacts abroad were accidentally purchased * Second: By agreement or negotiation to reclaim these artifacts * Third: Make legal claims.
Mr. Long Ponna Sereywat added that from 1996 to the present, Cambodia has demanded the return of more than 600 lost artifacts from abroad.
