Find out about the United Nations:
The United Nations (UN) was established on October 24, 1945, with 51 members, but no headquarters at that time. It was not until December 11, 1945, that the United States Congress unanimously voted in favor of New York City.
* Member countries
* Directions and principles
The UN Charter contains 111 articles. Article 1 and Article 2 state its direction and principles.
The United Nations has six main bodies: the General Assembly, the Secretariat, the Security Council, the International Court of Justice, and the Economic and Social Council.
1. General Assembly The Annual General Meeting is a forum for member countries to discuss and adopt various resolutions every September. The official languages used at the convention are English, French, Spanish, Arabic, Russian and Chinese.
The Security Council, as the executive branch, has a role to play in promoting world peace. The five permanent members of the Security Council, the superpowers, had a consensus principle when it came to achieving a will, but today the superpowers never adhere to that principle.
If any member state involved in the dispute refuses to comply with the Security Council resolution, the Council will take two actions:
* Non-use of sanctions - Diplomatic talks - Economic sanctions, severance of international economic ties, closure of land and air, leading to social and economic crisis in the country.
* Punishment by using force to send Blue Hat troops to blockade, send warships into waters, or launch air strikes to pressure the country to accept Security Council conditions, such as the 1991 Persian Gulf War. Nations against Iraq refusing to withdraw troops from Kuwait.
3: International Court The International Court of Justice has 15 judges with a term of 9 years, elected by the General Assembly 1/3 (5 people) every 3 years. The judges who are elected are people of good morals, according to the conditions of a country, choose a judge.
4. The United Nations Secretariat is chaired by the Secretary-General for a five-year term, elected by the General Assembly. There have been nine UN Secretary-Generals from 1945 to 2020, from Trivia to Antonio Guterres.
5. Economic and Social Council This council has 54 members and regularly meets twice a year to discuss economic issues and aid to third countries. The council promotes the livelihood of the people, develops the world economy and monitors human rights with other affiliated organizations such as the International Labor Organization, the World Food Program and the International Monetary Fund.
6. Board of Directors This council is responsible for the administration of the former colonies of Italy, Germany and Japan.
