Russia: More than 1,300 arrested in anti-conspiracy protests
LONDON (Reuters) - Russian security forces detained more than 1,300 people on Wednesday, just hours after a rally against Vladimir Putin ordered the first arrests. Since World War II. This is according to human rights organizations quoted by Reuters on Thursday, September 22, 2022.
Among those released were citizens from the United States, Britain and Morocco, as well as those who were sentenced to death by Russia after their capture in Ukraine. Accused by Moscow of being a mercenary.
Russia has also released 215 Ukrainian prisoners of war, five of them commanders who led the fight against the Russian army in Mariupol, and in response Ukraine has released a total of 55 Russian troops and pro-Moscow separatists. The exchange of prisoners of war took place under the coordination of Saudi Arabia and Turkey.
