Japanese PM talks about helping bring peace to Cambodia at UN General Assembly
(Phnom Penh) - Japanese Prime Minister Kishida highlighted some of the reasons for Japan's contribution to the search for peace 30 years ago at the 77th United Nations General Assembly in New York.
The Japanese Embassy in Cambodia quoted Kishida as saying, "Japan has also made a long-term contribution to peacebuilding. Japan first joined the full peacekeeping mission in Cambodia in 1992. Thirty years later, many Cambodian Blue Hat troops are defending peace and the future of places like Mali, the Central African Republic and Lebanon. ”
"The light of peace that has been created through Japan's contribution to peacekeeping operations will be passed down from generation to generation and extended to other countries," Kishida stressed. "Japan continues to support this action."
Please note that 2022 is the 30th anniversary of Japan sending peacekeepers to complete the peace mission in Cambodia. It was also the time when Japan sent its first peacekeepers abroad.
Two Japanese citizens were killed during a peacekeeping mission in Cambodia. The Cambodian people will always remember Japan's contribution to the search for peace in Cambodia. This year, Cambodia and Japan have organized many events to commemorate this meaningful past.
