Ms. Ing Kantha Phavi, Minister of Women's Affairs, in a press conference of the Royal Government Spokesperson's Unit on the morning of September 21, 2022, emphasized that in addition to the telephone numbers provided, victims in need of intervention can also contact The Ministry of Women's Affairs can do the same or ask for help from the nearest competent authority.
The Minister acknowledged that in the past, the use of social media has been very helpful in providing the Ministry with access to information on requests for assistance, but there is also some information that is fabricated with the same bad intentions. She added that when receiving information, the Ministry of Women's Affairs must research to find a clear source in advance to facilitate the intervention, and not all cases are resolved quickly.
"Most of the public wants to see the Ministry intervene immediately within 24 hours, but we can not, because the search for justice must be done with real research," said Ing Kantha Phavi. We intervene in the investigation first, contact the special authorities with the General Commission of the National Police to determine whether this problem occurs or not? "Sometimes they just invent."
Regarding this case of requesting for intervention, Ms. Sineth, Deputy Director General of the Director General of Social Development, Ministry of Women's Affairs, said that at present, most of the intervention for women, as well as the victims themselves, is done through social media or Run to the local authorities nearby, but all need to use the time, not immediately at the will of the victim.
Ms. Sineth added that the mechanism by which the Ministry of Women's Affairs has responded most importantly is that the ministry has a multidisciplinary response team on violence involving women from the ministry to the district level, as well as the HelpLine. Also. By setting up a telephone number to provide assistance, this intervention is making it easier for the victims and ensuring their safety.
Please be informed that in the two years (2019-2022), the Ministry of Women's Affairs had 3,1153 women victims of gender-based violence, received psychological counseling services, facilitated finding lawyers, prepared complaints, intervened and facilitated referrals. And monitor verdict announcements directly and online.
