General knowledge: The roots of Pchum Ben are related to Preah Ko temple
(Phnom Penh): According to the historical records of Cambodia, the Pchum Ben festival has existed since ancient times, that is, at the beginning of the Angkorian period, during the reign of King Indravarman I, who reigned in the capital Hariharalaya from the 8th century AD. 77-889.
According to the inscription, King Indravarman I held a royal ceremony to cross the Preah Ko temple on Monday, January 25, 880, and it is believed that a magical temple was erected at the shrine to pay homage to the ancestors. His people who have passed away.
Historians claim that the Passover is a spiritual ritual that continues to this day and has become a tradition in the Khmer Pchum Ben festival today.
The location of Preah Ko temple is in Bakong commune, Prasat Bakong district, Siem Reap province. If traveling from Phnom Penh, passengers must turn off National Road 6 to Bakong temple. When approaching Bakong temple, travelers will see Preah Ko temple on the right hand side. .
