The garment sector has contributed significantly to Cambodia's economic growth, with garment exports accounting for more than 40% of Cambodia's total exports in the first eight months of 2022.
Meanwhile, the Garment Manufacturers Association of Cambodia (GMAC) has expressed concern about the decline in garment orders in the second half due to global economic uncertainty.
He said the reason for the decline was due to Cambodia's major export markets, such as Europe, which is in a bad state and the global economic situation is not good, while buyers have a lot of goods and not yet sold out. Lead them to reduce orders during this period. This factor may affect garment exports in the second half of this year and next year as well.
According to Ken Loo, in the first half of 2022, garment exports increased by more than 40%, with the main export markets being the United States, followed by the European Union, Japan, the United Kingdom and Canada.
In 2021, Cambodia exported garments totaling 8,017 million US dollars, an increase of 8.53% compared to 2020, which was only 7,387 million US dollars. Agricultural community encourages farmers to reconsider mango care while mangoes are good value
The low price of this type of mango is due to the fact that mangoes are not abundant, while farmers start to peel mangoes or mangoes for sale before the harvest season, keeping only the first quality mangoes or packaged mangoes. On the stems for sale at harvest time.
She said the mango was being valued, with many brokers vying to buy it from farmers for resale. While the mango is being harvested, farmers are not selling it to traders because some farmers have cut down the trees and some farmers do not continue to care after they suffered severely during the Kovid 19 outbreak, which caused prices. Falling prices.
Ms. Chhin Chamroeun wants farmers who have this mango on the tree should sell it from time to time, if left until it is old enough to harvest, the price of mangoes will fall sharply.
She hopes that by the end of 2022, the mango harvest season, mango prices will continue to rise. Therefore, she encourages farmers to try to improve the quality of their mangoes, and some farmers who have cut down mango trees or stopped maintenance should start over.
