Understand business: Experts suggest that in the current economic situation, low- and middle-income earners want to invest no more than 30% of their income and are encouraged to invest in real estate.
(Phnom Penh): A well-known real estate expert in Cambodia advises low and middle income people if they want to invest, do not invest more than 30% of their monthly income and encourage investment In the real estate sector, it is better to buy a home if there is no need to buy one.
Mr. Oknha Nuon Rithy said that in the past, homebuyers who bought houses to invest for profit because they could borrow from the bank with the money to buy a house to book 5 houses and pay in installments later and then at the time. Good economy.
He said that the phase of the Cambodian economy in the next three years and will continue to be unpredictable due to the Kovid-19 disease and the war crisis, as well as the division of the bloc of the superpowers affect the economy. Round and affect the flow of Cambodia's economy as well.
Housing is an option, and before thinking about housing, think about feeding, educating children. At this stage, it can be seen that the builders are not selling well and very few are able to sell because of the need to buy a house.
Medium and low-cost housing, on the other hand, is also not located in urban areas, mostly in the suburbs or suburbs, so some people do not want the demand, unlike when the economy is good. Everyone thinks that anywhere is easy to sell.
"For low- and middle-income people, there is a greater chance of buying land for profit than buying low- and middle-income housing, because developers have already taken into account that living on average housing costs can take time," he said. "It takes between three and five years to sell at a profit, according to economic development, and the rise in house prices is due to land."
Mr. Oknha said that for low and middle income people in the face of such economic situation, if they want to invest, they have to spend only 15-30% of their income.
He observes that some investors buy by throwing money, so sometimes in areas that do not have potential for 10 years, there is no odds. Some people invest in land that falls under state territory, which is a problem. At the same time, he believes that at this stage, all banks also have restrictions related to housing financing and land investors.
