Southeast Asia needs $ 210 billion annual investment in renewable energy (Video inside) (Jakarta): Southeast Asian countries need more than double their annual investment in renewable energy to Accelerate energy exchange and to achieve its climatic goals. According to Reuters.
Reuters reports, citing a report released Thursday by the International Renewable Energy Agency (IRENA) that in the long run, an average annual investment of $ 210 billion is needed for renewable energy, energy efficiency and sustainable development. To support technology and infrastructure during the period up to 2050 to achieve the target of a global temperature rise of 1.5 degrees Celsius.
According to Reuters, Southeast Asia is a source of 25 percent of the world's geothermal capacity, but also has significant coal reserves. Indonesia, the region's largest economy, is the world's largest exporter of thermal coal.
While half of the members of the Association of Southeast Asian Nations (ASEAN) have vowed to stop using coal in the energy sector, La Camera said climate commitment requires "immediate action" that must begin now. This is to be successful.
According to IRENA, Southeast Asia aims to achieve 23% of its primary energy supply from renewable energy by 2025, although investment in recent years has shown mixed progress. According to Reuters.
Sri Lanka's economy shrinks 8.4 percent in second quarter, video data show (Sri Lanka) Two years compared to the same period last year as the country struggles with its worst financial crisis in more than seven decades. According to Reuters.
Severe dollar shortages due to mismanagement of the economy and the effects of the Kovid-19 pandemic have left Sri Lankans short of money to pay for imports of key commodities, including food, fuel, fertilizers and medicines. Doctor.
The data also showed that the island nation of 22 million people saw a 1.6 percent contraction during the first quarter of 2022.
The Sri Lankan Census and Statistics Department said agriculture fell 8.4 percent in the second quarter and industry fell 10 percent, while services fell 2.2 percent from a year earlier. Before.
Sri Lanka's central bank has forecast that the country's economy will shrink by about 8 percent by 2022. According to Reuters.
