CLMV holds 14th meeting to discuss ways to become a prosperous country
Pen Sovicheat, Undersecretary of State and spokesman for the Ministry of Commerce, said at a press conference on the evening of September 16 that at the ministerial meeting, it was noted that the four countries had progressed at a lower rate than some countries. In addition, the economies of the four countries will reach more than 748 billion US dollars in 2021, an increase of 20 percent compared to 2020. Vietnam has the highest investment volume at 15.6 billion USD.
At the same time, all ministers urged the implementation of the CLMV Action Plan 2021-2022. The plan encourages the organization of exhibitions, events and promotes investment and trade between the four countries, as well as provides technical support on issues such as credit guarantee systems, investment, etc., all of which are supported by Some countries, such as Japan, show interest in small and medium enterprises.
Mr. Pen Sovicheat said that the four ministers have put in place action plans for 2023-2024, focusing on six areas: trade, cooperation, investment, digital economy, implementation of all commitments promised in the rehabilitation plan area. Post-crisis economy 19, especially plans to implement the CLMV development framework and human resource development for the four countries.
At the same time, it also adopted an action plan to help each other achieve the CLMV Vision 2030, which all countries aim to transition from low-income to high-middle-income countries by 2030. In particular, Cambodia intends to push itself out of the status of less developed countries.
Putin and Xi: It's time to restructure the international system to push the world order in a more right and rational way
(Uzbekistan): Russian President Vladimir Putin and Chinese President Xi Jinping called for a change in world order to challenge Western influence during a meeting with Asian leaders on Friday. This is according to the release by AFP on Friday, September 16, 2022.
The meeting of the Shanghai Cooperation Organization (SCO) in Uzbekistan, the former Soviet Union, brought Putin and Xi together with leaders of other SCO members, including India, Pakistan and four Central Asian countries, as well as the presidents of Iran and Iran. Turkey too.
Putin and Xi met on Thursday for their first face-to-face talks since the beginning of the conflict in Ukraine and on the first visit by a Chinese leader abroad. From the first days of the Kovid-19 plague.
The summit was seen as an option for "Western-oriented organizations" amid mounting pressure on Moscow over Ukraine and anger in Beijing over US support for Taiwan.
Xi Jinping told leaders at the summit that it was time to reorganize the international system, abandoning the zero-sum game and bloc politics.
"Leaders should work together to promote the development of the international order in the right direction and more rationally," Xi said.
Putin, meanwhile, hailed the country's growing influence outside the West, criticizing what he called "protectionism, illegal sanctions and economic selfishness."
