ASEAN-Korea economic ministers see importance of boosting bilateral trade ties, ASEAN-Korea trade to reach $ 189.5 billion by 2020 (Video inside) (Siem Reap): The ASEAN Economic Ministers and the Republic of Korea will be convened on September 16, 2022, chaired by Cambodian Minister of Commerce Pan Sosak and South Korean Minister of Commerce.
In this regard, both sides appreciated the importance of the relationship and noted that despite the 19-crisis Kovid, both sides made efforts to reopen the ASEAN-Korea market through the use of vaccines, and the Korean side Vaccinated ASEAN as well.
The meeting considered and appreciated that without the use of vaccines, it is impossible to reopen the market and not be able to promote economic relations as it is now.
Mr. Pen Sovicheat said that the meeting also welcomed and reviewed the implementation of the ASEAN-Korea Free Trade Agreement to review the ASEAN-Korea trade relations, including the reduction of tariffs, elimination of non-tariff goods, facilitation. Trade and other jobs involve non-tax barriers. In particular, it is open to the review of sensitive goods.
The meeting between the ASEAN Economic Ministers and the Korean Economic Ministers also discussed the use of technology, in which five ASEAN countries have already selected Korean technology, including Burma, Cambodia, Indonesia, Thailand and the Philippines.
ASEAN-Korea also agreed to establish a technology hub in the future to bring about technological change in industry and innovation.
This was confirmed by Mr. Pen Sovichet, Spokesman of the ASEAN Economic Ministers' Meeting and related meetings hosted by Cambodia in Siem Reap, in a press conference after the meeting on the afternoon of September 16. This 2022.
In this meeting, the whole meeting noted that the economic relations between ASEAN and India have been steadily emerging, especially the recovery of relations between the two countries after the 19-crisis Kovid, which has seen a steady increase so far. To 39.2 percent compared to last year in 2021, an increase of 91.5 percent.
At the same time, the ASEAN and Indian Economic Ministers acknowledged that after Kovid 19, the economic crisis persisted and we were ready to address all the challenges facing the region, including the world.
Both sides expressed concern over geopolitical issues such as global food shortages and rising consumer prices, inflation in general.
At the same time, of further interest, both Ministers, as well as all ASEAN Ministers, considered that in the aftermath of the Kovid 19 crisis, it was necessary to have a specific course of action to address these challenges and reconnect supply ties.
The meeting decided to fully support the review of ASEAN-India trade relations, a trade agreement.
In order for this agreement to be effective, it is necessary to establish a working group that will monitor and coordinate with representatives from all countries, including India, to jointly consider what issues are unresolved and Must be implemented in a timely manner.
At the same time, the Ministers of ASEAN and India welcomed the growing ties and activities between the ASEAN-India Business Council and urged the Council to pay close attention to the recovery of small and medium enterprises. And micro-enterprises of 11 countries.
