The Association of Banks and Microfinance of Cambodia issues a joint statement on the research report on "Microfinance in Cambodia: Development, Challenges and Recommendations"
(Phnom Penh): The Association of Banks in Cambodia and the Association of Microfinance in Cambodia have expressed some concerns over a research report on "Microfinance in Cambodia: Development, Challenges and Recommendations" under the auspices of the German Federal Ministry for Foreign Affairs. Economic Cooperation (BMZ), although some studies show the positive aspects of microfinance lending in Cambodia, improve the lives of Cambodians.
According to a joint press release between the Association of Banks in Cambodia and the Microfinance Association of Cambodia, obtained by Fresh News on September 16, 2022, citing the contents of the research study from this report that the microfinance sector In Cambodia, it has made a huge positive contribution and improved the lives of Cambodians who are customers of banking and financial services, especially in terms of increasing income and land ownership.
In addition to the positive effects cited, the study also argues that credit is hardly a major cause of child labor or migration. On the contrary, most credit customers enjoy many positive benefits, including increased income and land ownership.
Both associations perceive that the boundaries of this study do not fully reflect the overall picture of the sector. This may be due to different definitions of the term "excessive debt" or to different aspects of the microfinance context in Cambodia.
A press release from the Association of Banks in Cambodia and the Association of Microfinance of Cambodia added: "By not presenting the main content of the study report, which has caused confusion among the public."
In addition to the above measures and initiatives, the Association will invite an independent international institution to conduct an in-depth study of the overall picture of the microfinance sector.
