Residents of Run Ta Ek Natural Village have a comfortable life
(Siem Reap): Residents who volunteered to leave the Angkor resort area to live in Run Ta Ek Natural Village confirmed that they have a comfortable daily life because they have land for farming and use as needed.
Rin Sruoch, a former resident of Srah Srong village, Nokor Thom commune, Siem Reap city, said that when he lived in the Angkor resort, he had a small plot of land that was not legally owned and he could not take full advantage of the land. It is possible that his family volunteered to trade with the APSARA Authority from the protected area of Angkor to live in Run Ta Ek Natural Village.
He said that now he has prepared a house as a guest house for tourists by day and month, and he receives a decent monthly income from the house and farm yields. He is happy because living in Run Ta Ek Natural Village allows him to do whatever he wants in his daily life.
Ms. Ke Kinrey also said that she volunteered to move to Run Ta Ek Natural Village for 5 years. Since she moved in, she has been cultivating crops such as coconuts, papayas, pineapples, lemons and raising cattle, chickens, ducks and geese. Today, Kinrey owns 13 cows and more than 100 chickens, ducks and geese.
Every day, she can earn at least 30,000 riel per day from selling lemons, duck eggs and chicken eggs. In particular, during the goose egg season, he can earn a considerable daily income by selling goose eggs, which makes life easier.
For the current business, many families, some families have joined the APSARA Authority, some are engaged in building houses for tourists and others are farming on their own land, which is why they earn a lot of money each month. . World Ozone Day 16 September! Say Sam Al believes that with the participation of all nations, the ozone layer may return to normal by the middle of the 21st century.
He said that due to this effective global multilateral cooperation, the ozone layer is now continuously improving and can play a central role in protecting humanity from the sun's ultraviolet rays, especially protecting millions of people. From skin cancer and cataracts.
Mr. SAY Sam Al emphasized that as a State Party, the Kingdom of Cambodia actively participates in this global effort by completely eliminating the use of chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs) in 2010 and is continuing to implement the Hydrochloride Management Plan. Fluorocarbons (HCFCs) comply with the obligations set forth in the Montreal Protocol.
Reducing the use of HFCs under the Kigali Amendment, along with the shift to energy-efficient refrigeration equipment, will double the benefits of global warming and provide many socio-economic benefits. And the environment as well. This is a win-win strategy for the Cambodian economy and for tackling global climate change.
