(Banteay Meanchey): Authorities in Poipet Municipality, Banteay Meanchey Province, inspected the flood situation and brought water rescue equipment caused by continuous rain to prevent flooding of people's houses, schools, pagodas and construction sites. Stung Bot Gate Administration Building is located in Sangkat Phsar Kandal.
The operation was carried out under the direction of Deputy Prime Minister Ke Kim Yan, Chairman of the Royal Government Working Group to assist Banteay Meanchey province to solve the flood problem in a timely manner.
In fact, on September 13, 2022, Mr. Keat Hul, Governor of Poipet City, accompanied by Mr. Keo Dararasmey, Deputy Governor, Mr. Uy Chanrith, Permanent Member of the Municipal Party and Chairman of the Poipet City Youth Working Group, led the team to inspect the flood situation. Flooded houses and some places in Stung Bot village, Sangkat Phsar Kandal, Poipet city, Banteay Meanchey province.
After examining the actual situation, Mr. Keat Hul discussed with the construction team of the administrative building and the Stung Bot International Gate Road, and decided to install drainage equipment to release water from the area to avoid flooding people's houses, schools, pagodas and the construction site of the administrative building. The Stung Bat gate is also open.
On September 14, 2022, the Ministry of Water Resources and Meteorology presented the water situation along the border in Banteay Meanchey province and confirmed that there is no flood in this province, especially along the border line.
In connection with this situation, Mr. Lim Kean Hor, Minister of Water Resources and Meteorology, ordered the Department of Water Resources and Meteorology to cooperate with the competent authorities to regularly monitor the water level and to report to the Ministry for immediate response. In case of any need, especially floods.
In addition, the Ministry of Water Resources and Meteorology also called on people to cooperate with the relevant authorities on a regular basis to monitor the water in their local area, especially to take good care of children to avoid accidents due to floods. .
