The National Bank of Cambodia and JICA cooperate on capacity building for research, economic analysis and monetary transactions
(Phnom Penh): The National Bank of Cambodia on September 13, 2022 signed the Technical Cooperation Project between the National Bank of Cambodia and the Japan International Cooperation Agency (JICA) on “Improving Research Capacity”. "Economic analysis and monetary transactions."
Technical cooperation will last for three years from 2023 to 2025, which will enhance the effectiveness of the National Bank of Cambodia's monetary policy by improving interbank currency market operations and strengthening public relations. To raise awareness of monetary policy practices.
At the same time, the technical cooperation will also increase the human resource capacity of the National Bank of Cambodia in analyzing the economic and monetary situation in Cambodia, while this technical project will contribute to the development of monetary policy mechanisms and frameworks to support the improvement of the monetary policy. Policy decisions.
The National Bank of Cambodia highly appreciates JICA's contribution to socio-economic development and, in particular, the continued strengthening of past technical cooperation that has contributed to the development of the banking and financial sector in Cambodia. Senior Minister Sun Chanthol leads a delegation from the Ministry to bring candles and relics, including suffixes, to 7 pagodas in Siem Reap province
(Siem Reap): Senior Minister Sun Chanthol, Minister of Public Works and Transport, on September 13, 2022, led a delegation of the Ministry to bring candles and relics, as well as offerings to monks at 7 pagodas, including: Wat Preah Brahma Roth. Wat Preah Reach Damnak, Wat Preah Reach Bo, Wat Indabat Borey Angkor South, Wat Indabat Borey Angkor North, Wat Preah Ang Kork Thlok and Preah Ang Chek Preah Ang Cham Shrine in Siem Reap City, Siem Reap Province.
The offering of Vesa candles and relics was also attended by the leaders of the Ministry of Public Works and Transport and the Buddhists at the foot of the pagoda near and far, and there were Buddhist ceremonies such as: worship of Preah Ratanatray Samathan Sil Then, candles, pottery and equipment were also given to the monks.
The ceremony is designed to provide monks with the necessities of life while the monks are waiting for the rain for three months during the rainy season, without allowing them to travel far and wide during the day and night, except for important tasks such as Parents who are seriously ill or are invited to give a sermon, determined to return in seven days, is an important time for monks and nuns to study the Dharma and Discipline in accordance with the Buddha's teachings.
Ministry of Environment and Ministry of Education jointly launch strategic initiatives on climate change and education to develop human resources ready to respond to climate change
(Phnom Penh): The Ministry of Environment and the Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports have jointly announced the launch of a strategic initiative on climate change and education to develop human resources ready to respond to climate change by raising awareness of climate change. Climate change and training arrangements get better results.
For the main contents of the joint declaration between the Ministry of Environment and the Ministry of Education, including:
* Continue to integrate climate change into political programs, strategies and national action plans, as well as development plans and budgets in the education sector at both the national and sub-national levels. And capacity building on climate change in educational institutions at all levels.
* Prepare documents and materials on climate change as needed for teachers and students at all levels (at the primary, secondary and tertiary levels).
* Continuing to integrate climate change into postgraduate and postgraduate curricula (university curricula), especially the development of subjects (master's degree) on climate change.
* Develop creative materials for students' "extracurricular" learning on climate change and environmental issues.
* Extensively promote the concept of eco-friendly schools, especially the establishment of eco-clubs where students can increase their understanding of climate change and environmental education in their schools and communities. Assess vulnerabilities for the education sector as part of the implementation of the National Contribution Update (Updated NDC) under the United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change.
Promote competition programs in educational institutions, especially universities, on adaptation activities and reduce greenhouse gas emissions.
Develop data collection indicators for impact monitoring and adaptation to climate change in schools and communities, such as droughts, heat waves, floods and hurricanes, to avoid impacts and improve resilient school infrastructure. And weather (such as equipping and accessing clean water for use and sanitation, addressing high temperature rise through tree planting, fan use, and protection of the stomach, etc.).
In addition, the Ministry of Environment and the Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports will continue to monitor the latest developments in climate change, as well as jointly strengthen good cooperation to implement the above measures effectively and efficiently.
