Japanese Foreign Minister hands over letters of appreciation to two Cambodian and Japanese dignitaries for their work in promoting mutual understanding between Japan and Cambodia
(Phnom Penh): The Ministry of Foreign Affairs of Japan awarded a letter of commendation to Dr. Nao Thuok, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, and Mr. Yamada Yositaka, Advisor to the Royal Government of Cambodia, for their remarkable achievements in promoting mutual understanding between Japan and the Kingdom of Cambodia will contribute to the goodwill friendship between Japan and other countries.
The handover ceremony took place on the night of September 9, 2022 in the presence of Mr. Mikami Masahiro, Ambassador of Japan to Cambodia, Mr. Mey Kalyan, Senior Advisor of the Supreme National Economic Council, General Sem Sovanny, Director General The National Center for the Management of Peacekeeping Forces, Demining and Remnants of War (NPMEC), including a number of government officials of the Royal Government of Cambodia, at the Japanese Embassy.
In addition, when Japan provided technical cooperation in the fisheries sector, Dr. Nao Thuok acted as a facilitator for the Royal Government of Cambodia and made efforts to implement various technical cooperation, including the establishment of Marine Aquaculture Development Center in Sihanoukville. I have heard that Cambodians get most of their protein from fisheries and the fishing industry is an important industry in Cambodia.
In addition, Mr. Mikami Masahiro confirmed that Dr. has been acting as a bridge between Japan and Cambodia in the fisheries sector for many years.
In addition, since 2008, Mr. Yamada Yosita Taka has been the International Advisor of the National Center for the Management of Peacekeeping Forces, Demining and Remnants of War (NPMEC) and since 2012 As an advisor to the Royal Government of Cambodia, his current position.
As an NPMEC Advisor, Yamada has actively promoted cooperation between Japan and Cambodia in the field of peacekeeping operations (PKO). Due to the efforts of HE Yamada, many Self-Defense Forces have been sent to Cambodia and have been continuously conducting training on road maintenance and surveillance.
The Cambodian trainees, trained by Japan, are currently deployed to Africa and other countries as PKO forces and are contributing to world peacekeeping using the knowledge they have acquired. Through training.
Overall, the efforts of Dr. Nao Thuok and Mr. Yamada Yosita Taka have contributed to the further development of bilateral relations between Japan and Cambodia.
The European Union (EU), which brings together EU member states on Friday, formally approved a decision to suspend the visa agreement from September 12, after it had been in force for more than a year. Since 2007. The Visa Coordinating Agreement provides for the privilege of entering the EU for citizens of a trusted partner country.
The move would increase the visa application fee for Russian citizens from 35 euros ($ 35) to 80 euros, requiring more documentary evidence, longer filing times, and more. Stricter rules for multiple entry visas.
Russia on Thursday warned against responding to the move but would not completely close its borders to the EU. Last week, EU foreign ministers agreed to suspend the visa agreement instead of the option of imposing a visa ban on all Russians, as called by Ukraine and some EU members.
Some EU countries, including Estonia, Latvia and Lithuania, have decided to ban all Russians from entering their country, either directly from Russia or Belarus, from mid-September, with some exceptions. For diplomats and truck drivers.
