(Phnom Penh): The Royal Government of Cambodia commends the Siem Reap provincial authorities and the Ministry of Interior for working together to resolve a rally of LDP activists at the foothills of Kulen pepper plantation in Banteay Srei district, Siem Reap province. No violence.
This praise was made in the meeting of the Council of Ministers this morning, September 9, 2022, chaired by Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia.
It should be noted that under the superstition propaganda of world disasters, earthquakes and floods by Mr. Khem Veasna, President of the LDP party, made a total of more than 10,000 activists, members of this party members gathered at the pepper plantations at the foot of the mountain. Kulen, Banteay Srey District, Siem Reap Province.
The authorities demanded that they disperse on the grounds that it was against the law. Under the efforts and coordination of the authorities, the LDP activists agreed to disband. So far, only a handful of LDP activists remain in pepper plantations, while Khem Veasna and some of his allies continue to propagate the threat of a global catastrophic flood.
The Royal Government has ordered the Capital-Provincial Administration to launch a campaign to crack down on crime, drugs and human trafficking (Video inside) (Phnom Penh): The Royal Government of Cambodia has issued strict orders to all Capital, Provincial and local administrations and competent institutions. Involved in launching effective campaigns to combat and suppress drug crime, trafficking and smuggling, forced labor and sex trade.
This regulation was issued at a cabinet meeting chaired by Cambodian Prime Minister Samdech Techo Hun Sen this morning, September 9, 2022.
The Royal Government of Cambodia's order comes as drug crime appears to be on the rise in Cambodia. Authorities cracked down on large-scale drug trafficking and drug production.
In particular, human trafficking, forced labor, etc. were also found to be hot issues in Cambodia until the United States downgraded Cambodia. Recently, the Ministry of Interior has also launched a campaign to clean up human trafficking, detention and forced labor in many cases.
The issuance of the Royal Government's regulations today is a demonstration of the Royal Government's willingness to address the above issues in accordance with the wishes of the people.
