China's exports to Russia, hit by Western sanctions over its invasion of Ukraine, have risen, according to Reuters' calculations based on Chinese customs data on Wednesday. Up 26.5 percent against the dollar from a year earlier, up from 22.2 percent in July this year.
Imports from Russia rose 59.3 percent, compared with a 49.3 percent increase in July.
Russia is an important source of oil, gas, coal and agricultural products for China.
Russia was one of China's largest oil suppliers for the third straight month in July, according to Chinese customs data released on Aug. 20. The customs agency has not yet released the energy supply country rankings for August.
Russia's Gazprom said on Tuesday it had signed an agreement to begin shifting payments for gas supplies to China to the yuan and the ruble instead of the dollar.
In the first eight months of this year, bilateral trade between China and Russia rose 31.4 percent to 117.2 billion US dollars. According to Reuters.
