(Phnom Penh): Lieutenant Colonel Kasy, an assistant of the Dangkor District Gendarmerie, was on duty to facilitate traffic and protect the procession of street leaders when he was hit by a tuk-tuk driving carelessly, causing him to break his head and bleed. Fell to the ground, seriously injured.
The owner of the tuk-tuk panicked because he made a mistake that affected the officers who were on duty and could not afford to pay for the treatment of the victim, so he received a favor from Lt. Gen. Rath Sreang, Deputy Commander of the Royal Gendarmerie. Nationally and as the Commander of the Phnom Penh Gendarmerie, he did not punish the owner of the tricycle.
In addition to not committing the crime of dragging the trip, Lt. Gen. Rath Sreang also did not pay for the medical treatment of the victims, but only acknowledged the mistakes he had made and received training from the traffic law not to be neglected in the future. The victim was sent by the lieutenant general to the Cambodia-China Friendship Hospital in Preah Kosomak.
