The move comes as observers say North Korea appears to be resuming its first nuclear test since 2017. The North's parliament passed the law on Thursday, the state-run KCNA news agency reported Friday.
Speaking to lawmakers, Kim Jong Un stressed that "the importance of this law is to set the necessary conditions for non-negotiation of our nuclear program." The law also prohibits the sharing of nuclear weapons or technology with other countries.
In the past, North Korea has already designated the country as a nuclear weapons state in its constitution, but the new law further specifies when to use nuclear weapons, including responding to attacks or stopping aggression. Any. It also allows for pre-emptive nuclear strikes against a plot to use deadly weapons or against any "strategic target" of a country that has been discovered.
