Chea Chandara, President of the Association of Freight Forwarders and Supply Chains, told Fresh News on September 8 that since mid-2022, the cost of shipping by water, land and air has been steadily declining. So far, it has decreased by about 50% compared to 2020 and 2021.
He said that during the outbreak of Kovi 19 in 2020 and 2021, inland shipping from Cambodia to the United States and Europe increased between $ 17,000 and $ 20,000 per container. Containers due to container congestion in those countries. But now it has dropped to between $ 7,000 and $ 8,000 per container, depending on the ship and the shipping company.
He said the current drop in shipping costs was due to congestion of containers in various countries and the fall in international gasoline prices.
Air and land transportation are also better. According to the transport expert, after the outbreak of Kovid 19 in the past two years, air freight to Europe increased from $ 6 to $ 8 per kilogram. The gram dropped to more than $ 3 per kilogram, while shipments to the United States dropped from $ 9 to $ 5 per kilogram.
"We know that two months ago the price of oil was very high, but now, after the price of diesel, the price of land transport in Cambodia, we are also declining," he said. Therefore, his brothers find food in the transport sector, he can accept and can support his livelihood.
Chan Pich, general manager of Signature Fusa Asia, a group that exports agricultural products to European countries such as France, Spain and Italy and other countries, said that today shipping costs have dropped. About 15% or about $ 1,000 per container, which costs more than $ 5,000 to $ 6,000 per container.
He noted that shipping prices began to fall after sanctions imposed on Russia were eased.
He said the drop in shipping costs would make consumers more affordable through lower costs.
"Of course, shipping costs are the last resort on the market, so we see rising shipping costs pushing up inflation in the United States and the European Union," he added. .
