Cranes number declining for 3 consecutive years, Ministry of Environment, Ratanakkiri Provincial Administration and partner organizations are increasing conservation at Lumphat Wildlife Sanctuary
(Ratanakkiri): After the census found that the number of cranes has been declining continuously for the past three years, the Ministry of Environment, Ratanakkiri Provincial Administration and partner organizations have been increasing the conservation of cranes in the spawning area of Lumphat Wildlife Sanctuary is located in Kon Mom District, Rattanakiri Province.
The crane is a species of bird that is present in Southeast Asia and is listed as a rare and endangered species on the International Union for Conservation of Nature (IUCN Red List). According to expert studies, the number of cranes in the world today is between 13,000 and 15,000 living in India, Australia, Vietnam, Laos and Cambodia.
Regarding the declining situation of cranes, Mr. Net Pheaktra, Secretary of State and Spokesperson of the Ministry of Environment, led the leadership of the Ministry of Administration of Ratanakkiri Province, partner organizations, together with nearly 20 national and international journalists to directly inspect the spawning grounds. Of cranes next to Lumphat Wildlife Sanctuary, Kon Mom District, Ratanakkiri Province on September 3, 2022 recently.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra said that in Cambodia in 2020, according to the census, there were 194 cranes, but in 2021 we see a decrease of only 164, and in 2022, the report that we have not No official announcement has been made, but the number of cranes has dropped to just over 150.
A spokesman for the Ministry of Environment said that between June and October each year, the cranes 'spawning season and the grassland are cranes' nests. While cranes have appeared in some areas in Cambodia, such as the Eel Pring Protected Area in Kampot, the Boeung Prek Lpou Protected Area in Takeo Province and the Ang Trapeang Thmor Protected Area in Banteay Meanchey Province.
Meanwhile, the senior official added that for safety, the 270-hectare area has been designated as a conservation area for cranes' spawning grounds. 10 large poles and 70 small poles to indicate that these areas are areas that need to protect and conserve crane species.
He said the area was off-limits to domestic dogs because dogs could sniff and destroy cranes' eggs. The ministry also does not allow people to enter the spawning grounds indiscriminately during the period between June and October, which is the time for cranes to spawn and hatch.
According to Mr. Thol Sovannarith, Project Manager of Natural Life Cambodia, during the dry season, cranes move from their surroundings around the Tonle Sap Lake to the spawning grounds of the Lumphat Wildlife Sanctuary to be used for crane spawning. .
"Most of the failures in the conservation of cranes in the spawning area are caused by people bringing domestic dogs to hunt or take the cranes 'eggs because of cranes' nests," he said. "Easy to see, it broke the nest on the grass."
Mr. Sovannarith confirmed that the working group of the organization and the community has been working regularly to preserve and maintain the crane nests and crane habitats in the spawning area of the Lumphat Wildlife Sanctuary.
He stressed that this is a point to pay attention to the protection of crane habitat, especially in spawning areas. He said that the Ministry of Environment, as well as partner organizations and relevant institutions are studying the possibility of increasing the crane population through the use of methods such as increasing spawning through breeding to increase into the natural area.
