(Phnom Penh): National and international delegations attending the International Conference on "Prevention and Control of Illegal Cultural Trafficking: Perspectives from ASEAN" will visit the Koh Ker Temple and the Icon of Preah Skant Komar on September 8. 2022 In Preah Vihear province, a place where Khmer artifacts are stolen and traded illegally.
The visit to the cultural site was organized by the Ministry of Culture and Fine Arts, and according to officials at the Ministry of Culture, Fresh News reported that more than 100 national and international delegations will visit the Koh Ker temple and the icon of Preah Skant Komar.
An official from the Ministry of Culture added that "all participants will be able to reach the ancient sites that suffered from theft and vandalism caused by the illegal trade of artifacts in the cultural heritage sites."
The main purpose of the conference is to invite government officials, senior experts from around the world, including policy leaders, law enforcement, museums, art markets and civil society organizations to think together, create and explore strong mechanisms for Protecting the regional past for future generations and trying to reclaim ASEAN's national cultural heritage.
"Through the above international conference, we hope that the ASEAN nations and dialogue partners will not be indifferent if they see the cultural heritage of any country being sold in the international art market and help report or provide information to the countries concerned. "Reach out to take action to return to the country of origin peacefully." This is according to the official of the Ministry of Culture.
